<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="portfolio_contents_required">
  <title>
                Required Elements of the Portfolio
        </title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
                        The following items are required to be in your portfolio.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsubsection xml:id="self_eval">
    <title>
                        Reader's Guide and Progress Report
                </title>
    <p>
                        Depending on how you choose to organize your portfolio, this could simply be a table of contents plus a brief discussion of your progress.
    </p>
    <p>
                        For each submission of your portfolio, you will update your report to discuss your overall progress, to highlight progress since the last check-in, and to cite specific items of interest.
    </p>
    <p>
                        The goal of this is to help the reader (your instructor) prepare for the meeting with you and to make items easy to find.
    </p>
    <p>
                        One way to think of this is to think of answering the questions,
                                <ul>
                                        <li> <p> What are you particularly proud of or excited about?  </p> </li>
                                        <li> <p> What has been challenging?  </p> </li>
                                        <li> <p> What do you plan to do next?, and </p> </li>
                                        <li> <p> What do you need from me, the instructor, to facilitate your progress toward your goals?  </p> </li>
                                </ul>
    </p>
  </subsubsection>
  <subsubsection xml:id="port_progress_checks">
          <title>Progress Checks</title>
          <p>
                  Several times throughout the semester, we will have in-class progress checks. These are opportunities for you to show your understanding of concepts and fluency with calculations. 
          </p>
          <p>
                  When your progress checks are returned to you, you are expected to put them in your portfolio and write a reflection on your performance, noting things that went well as well as areas of future growth.
          </p>
          <p>
                  If there were substantial errors or misconceptions, those should also be addressed in the reflection.
          </p>
  </subsubsection>
  <subsubsection xml:id="project_catalog_of_examples">
    <title>Catalog of Detailed Examples</title>
    <p>
            Over the course of the semester we will be study functions and their derivatives from graphical, analytic, numerical, and conceptual viewpoints.
                For each topic, whenever possible, you must include at least one detailed example of the use of that concept in a practical setting. That is, each example must:
            <ul>
                    <li><p>Begin with a practical situation where the numbers have units and meaning.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Include a discussion of why the particular concept or tool was used (possibly including why other approaches would not be appropriate).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Show the process of using the concept to understand and solve the problem.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Include multiple representations, such as graphical, numerical, and algebraic representations.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Interpret the solution in context, giving a result with practical implications.</p></li>
            </ul>
    </p>
  </subsubsection>
  <subsubsection xml:id="portfolio_activities">
    <title> Overviews of Activities, Techniques, and Applications </title>
    <p>
        Over the course of the semester you will be assigned multiple activities, investigations, and projects dealing with techniques, concepts, and applications of calculus.
    </p>
    <p>
        For each <em>topic</em>  we study, not each activity, you are expected to demonstrate understanding of the main mathematical ideas and an ability to apply any associated techniques.
    </p>
    <p>
	Like everything in the portfolio, these are works in progress, and you are expected to revisit them in light of later activities or readings that may change your understanding of them.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>
  <subsubsection xml:id="sources">
    <title> Citation of Sources </title>
    <p>
        If you make use of any outside resources for this class, you are required to cite those sources. The particular format of citations is not important. If you need specificity, please follow MLA standards.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>
</subsection>
